WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.669
-I don't believe
this actually came out today,

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:04.136
but we were expecting that
Robert Mueller was gonna release

00:00:04.136 --> 00:00:07.173
the sentencing documents
for Michael Flynn today.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:08.741
Paul Manafort, we're hearing,
maybe Friday.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:11.044
These are called
public-facing documents?

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.346
-Yeah. It's bringing information
to the public

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.849
about why they're doing
what they've been doing.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:18.685
For instance, for Flynn,
it's about what Flynn

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.086
has been providing for them

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:22.488
since he's been cooperating
for a year.

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:24.790
I think they've delayed
his sentencing recommendation

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.392
for four different times,

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:27.560
'cause they were
still cooperating.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.162
So this will be whether or not

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:31.463
he thinks Flynn
should face any jail time

00:00:31.463 --> 00:00:33.766
and what
he's been doing to help.

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:35.969
-As a betting man,
do you think, in the end,

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.505
people will be satisfied
or disappointed?

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:40.373
Let's put it --
When I say "people,"

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:43.876
people who have been looking
to this Mueller investigation

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:46.279
as the moment that it will
all come crashing down.

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:49.649
Do you think they will be
disappointed or satisfied?

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:52.985
-Well, there's certain
left-leaning cable hosts

00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:55.855
that I think that
they will be very disappointed,

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.324
because it won't be, you know,
literally Donald Trump,

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:00.826
a picture with him
and a smoking gun and, you know,

00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:03.463
a tattoo of Vladimir Putin
on his chest.

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:05.230
But I do think it won't be good.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.599
-Yeah.
-I think it'll be ugly.

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:10.269
I don't think it will
necessarily affect him, per se.

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:11.503
And he won't be indicted,

00:01:11.503 --> 00:01:13.139
because there's
Justice Department guidelines

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:16.475
that sitting presidents are
seldom, I think, never indicted.

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:19.279
But, look, in the end,
here's the honest answer.

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:20.880
None of us have any idea.
-None of us have any idea.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.482
-Really. I mean, had you ever
heard of George Papadopoulos

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:23.650
before he was --

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.151
you know, before that
plea agreement came out?

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.219
-I had not, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.255
Tell me about this.
Last time you were here,

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:31.391
you were talking about your
"New York Times" best-selling

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:32.992
fiction book, "Hellfire Club."
-Yes.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.695
-You had written
a nonfiction book a ways back

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:37.030
called "The Outpost."
-Yes.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.164
-A wonderful, thrilling book

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:40.900
about a real-life incident
that happened.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.902
And now it's being adapted
into a film?

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.271
-That's right.
Rod Lurie, the director,

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:48.140
we just finished shooting
in Bulgaria,

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.209
where they had re-created
the entire Army base,

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:51.944
Combat Output Keating.

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:54.013
My family and I,
we went to go visit

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.648
and look at this Army base.

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:59.752
There were three actual veterans
who served at COP Keating.

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:00.786
It was attacked.

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:02.655
It was a horrible battle
in 2009.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:05.424
And they're working on the set.
I hooked them up with Rod.

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:07.927
And they're working on the set
and advising on the movie,

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:09.395
acting in the movie.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.063
And they were there,
and they were kind of, like,

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:13.065
spooked out
but also found it cathartic

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:15.968
that they were on this
recreation of a U.S. base

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:18.137
that had been attacked
and had been destroyed.

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:19.939
-And, obviously,
some lives were lost,

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:23.643
and this is a case
where now people are being --

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.277
some of those real-life people

00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:26.612
are being portrayed
in this film.

00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:28.847
-Yeah. So this is --
That's Rod Lurie, the director.

00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:30.082
This is Jacob Scipio.

00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:32.751
He's a British actor
who plays Justin Gallegos.

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:37.990
Justin was killed
on October 2, 2009.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.725
And he actually --

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:41.361
He was awarded, posthumously,
a Silver Star,

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:42.795
and the Army just announced

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:45.864
that they're upgrading it to
a Distinguished Service Cross.

00:02:45.864 --> 00:02:47.800
So, it's great.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.703
It's a way to honor these men
and their families.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.705
And I hope
it's meaningful for them.

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:54.440
It's been meaningful
to be part of it.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.509
-Another thing we talked about
is your daughter

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.377
wrote an op-ed
in "The New York Times."

00:02:58.377 --> 00:02:59.879
-NewYorkTimes.com, yes.
-Yes.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:02.281
-An op-ed about --
-And she is how old now?

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:04.384
-She's 11.
She was 10 when she --

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:05.885
when she and Bari Weiss,
the editor, worked on it.

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.319
-Good.
'Cause 11's a little late.

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:08.554
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:10.690
I'm glad she got it in at 10,
because, by 11,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.392
most people have written their
op-ed in "The New York Times."

00:03:13.392 --> 00:03:15.728
-She came up with this
great idea to encourage girls

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:16.962
to raise their hand in class.

00:03:16.962 --> 00:03:19.198
She noticed that boys
would raise their hand

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:20.866
even if they had no idea
what the answer was.

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:23.068
Squirrels!
They would -- you know.

00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:25.304
But girls didn't do --

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:27.906
Girls were very reticent,
very reluctant.

00:03:27.906 --> 00:03:29.909
And so she talked about it
with her Girl Scout troop.

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:31.344
She came up with the idea
for a patch,

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.145
a Raise Your Hand patch
to encourage girls

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:34.314
to raise their hand.

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:36.649
And I tweeted
the picture of the patch,

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:37.950
I was so excited,
and Bari Weiss

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.585
from "The New York Times"
saw it,

00:03:39.585 --> 00:03:42.355
and Alice wrote
an op-ed for "The Times."

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:45.691
-And now it's being adapted --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:45.691 --> 00:03:48.260
If you like that, you guys
are gonna be blown away by this.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.196
It's adapted into a book,
"Raise Your Hand."

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:51.764
-"Raise Your Hand."
Yeah.

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:54.867
This is --
It's so great, right?

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:56.202
It comes out in March.

00:03:56.202 --> 00:03:58.637
And it's basically
Alice's story.

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:00.273
Marta Kissi
did the illustrations.

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:02.942
They're amazing.
And, yeah, that's Alice.

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:04.476
That's what she looks like.
She's cuter than that.

00:04:04.476 --> 00:04:07.446
-That's fantastic.
[ Applause ]

00:04:07.446 --> 00:04:12.017
You are also an accomplished --
an accomplished artist.

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:13.586
You do the "State of Cartoonion"
every week.

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:15.154
-Yeah. "State of Cartoonion"
every week, yeah.

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:17.690
-Were you ever in the mix to
maybe do the art for this book?

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:18.857
-No.
Look how good this is.

00:04:18.857 --> 00:04:20.193
I mean, this is --
No, no, no.

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:22.362
They asked me.
-I mean, I'm not surprised.

00:04:22.362 --> 00:04:24.230
I also think this is better than
yours, but I was wondering...

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.531
[ Laughter ]

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:27.000
You knew right away that
you weren't gonna get the gig.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.535
-I didn't want to.
I'm not that good.

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.471
This us --
This is amazing stuff.

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:33.873
They did ask me
to do some illustrations

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.541
that are in the liner notes,

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:36.743
like, you know,
when you open the book

00:04:36.743 --> 00:04:38.378
before you get to the pages.

00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:40.213
It's kind of like
the very forgettable pages.

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:41.914
-Yeah. The one you flip through,
because you're like,

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:43.482
"This isn't the book yet."
-This isn't the book.

00:04:43.482 --> 00:04:44.617
I'm not in the book.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.285
The liner notes
have some illustrations.

00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:47.786
They're not in this copy here.

00:04:47.786 --> 00:04:49.922
They have some illustrations
that I did,

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:52.358
but I feel like they did that
to make me feel better.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.859
-Uh-huh, yeah.
Like they wanted

00:04:53.859 --> 00:04:55.261
to let you down easy
for the fact that --

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:56.829
-"Well, Alice has
a big book coming out,

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:58.664
and, you know,
but you can do the cartoons.

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:00.233
No, not the ones
that people will look at."

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:01.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.001
-Are you a little --
Obviously, you know,

00:05:03.001 --> 00:05:04.203
now you're both
in the book game.

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:05.271
Are you a little worried

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:06.539
more people are gonna buy
Alice's book?

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:09.509
-I hope more people
buy her book than mine.

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:12.011
Although, we should note,
all of the money,

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:13.278
Alice is donating all of it

00:05:13.278 --> 00:05:15.248
to the local chapter
of the Girl Scouts.

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:17.550
-That's fantastic.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:21.920
Because I'm pretty sure
with both of your books,

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.123
you famously kept all the money,
right?

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:27.293
-No, no.
For the novel I did,

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.594
but I did give a portion
of "The Outpost"

00:05:29.594 --> 00:05:31.297
to military charities.

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:32.498
-And let me just say real quick,

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:36.035
because you do so much
with military charities,

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:37.236
and you've helped me

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:38.437
get involved with some of them,
as well.

00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:39.471
I remember reaching out to you

00:05:39.471 --> 00:05:40.640
to ask what
some of the best are.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.308
-And every year,
when we do our auction,

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:44.443
you donate a visit
to the set here

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:46.279
and a photo-op for our auctions

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:47.880
to raise money
for Homes For Our Troops.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.715
-But you have been
so helpful with me.

00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:53.619
Do you feel like, right now,
obviously, we have --

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:56.221
And, again, we're talking about
when you mention John McCain,

00:05:56.221 --> 00:05:58.191
you mentioned George H.W. Bush.

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:00.359
You know, obviously,
they served this country.

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:02.094
We have a president
who talks a lot about

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:05.031
how he's the best president
for the military ever.

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:07.933
Real quick, is this country
doing enough for our vets?

00:06:07.933 --> 00:06:09.802
-Never. We're never
doing enough for our vets.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:11.070
I mean, the very fact
that a charity

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.472
like Homes For Our Troops
has to exist,

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:16.542
where it builds mortgage-free,
specially-designed homes

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.977
for the most
severely wounded veterans

00:06:18.977 --> 00:06:20.913
from Afghanistan and Iraq,

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.249
just so they can have some
semblance of a normal life,

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:25.117
the very fact that that charity
has to exist

00:06:25.117 --> 00:06:27.253
says we're not doing enough
for our veterans.

00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:28.821
But what I'm most
concerned about right now is,

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:30.989
I think we're actually now
at a point where

00:06:30.989 --> 00:06:38.330
more troops have killed
themselves, post-war,

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:42.267
than died in the first Iraq war
or in the Iraq war,

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:43.703
the most recent Iraq war,

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:45.872
because of the rate
of military suicides.

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:47.773
And so dealing with that
is something that I think

00:06:47.773 --> 00:06:49.908
society really
has to grapple with.

00:06:49.908 --> 00:06:51.177
-Well,
I certainly agree with you.

00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:53.445
And thank you for everything
you do for the vets,

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:54.646
and thank you for being here.

00:06:54.646 --> 00:06:55.715
It's always
such a pleasure to see you.

00:06:55.715 --> 00:06:57.583
-Happy Hanukkah.
-Happy Hanukkah to you.

